local _, ns = ...

--[[
	Content localization for the journal, in Russian.

	enUS.lua and ruRU.lua translate the interface; this file translates the content that lives
	in the data files (Data/Instances, Data/Authored, Data/Generated). Everything is keyed by the
	original English string, so the data files keep their own words and the layers do not fight:
	Core/Registry.lua's ns.ApplyLocale walks the registered instances, bosses, abilities, quests
	and transports after every data file has loaded and swaps in what is written here.

	  L.BOSS_NAMES     creature and boss names            English name   -> Russian
	  L.ABILITY_NAMES  spell names (a fallback: the client usually names spells itself)
	                                                      English name   -> Russian
	  L.STRINGS        free text: boss descriptions and notes, ability fight texts, phase and
	                   extra-ability titles, quest text, transport areas and destinations
	                                                      English phrase -> Russian

	Names of items and spells come from the game client and are not repeated here.

	By Staree4ok, for a client set to Russian; every other client skips it.
]]
-- The journal's words where it speaks this language; the tables matched against the client's own
-- text where the client does, previewed or not (ns.LocaleTargets, Locales/enUS.lua).
local L, client = ns.LocaleTargets("ruRU")
if not L then return end

